/*
 * nRF54L15DK dev-bench board (embarch-dev-bench/design.md §3 decision 26's
 * successor -- this board is the dev bench again as of 2026-08-31). Auto-
 * merged by Zephyr's board devicetree overlay convention (flat filename
 * matching the board target, same as this board's own .conf fragment and as
 * the ESP32-C5's overlay) -- no extra CMake wiring needed.
 *
 * The only thing this file has to say is the baud rate. `zephyr,console` on
 * this board is already `&uart20` (nrf54l_05_10_15_cpuapp_common.dtsi's own
 * chosen block), wired through the DK's onboard J-Link OB to VCOM0 -- so
 * unlike the ESP32-C5, which had to be moved off its native USB-Serial/JTAG
 * peripheral onto a separate bridge chip, nothing here needs repointing.
 * What does need overriding is `current-speed`: the board's upstream default
 * is 115200, and this link must match `embarch-core::dev_bench_link::
 * DEV_BENCH_BAUD` (1 Mbaud) exactly or Core's very first `Hello` fails to
 * decode.
 *
 * uart30 is left at its own 115200 default and unused. It is a real second
 * VCOM on this DK (VCOM1) and it is deliberately NOT given a role here:
 * app/prj.conf's decision-7 block turns off every log backend that would
 * write raw text, so there is nothing left that wants a second console, and
 * a second enabled UART would only add a candidate for
 * `embarch-topology`'s port detection to have to disambiguate.
 */

&uart20 {
	current-speed = <1000000>;
	/* **Hardware flow control, because this link silently loses bytes
	 * without it.** At 1 Mbaud the host driver's default ~4 KB receive
	 * buffer holds about 40 ms of traffic, so any stall longer than that on
	 * Core's side drops a contiguous run of bytes mid-frame. COBS resyncs at
	 * the next delimiter and the frame is simply gone; ordinary
	 * DevBenchMessages carry no CRC (only `Study` does), so nothing detects
	 * it except a decode failure. A 10 h PPG drain lost three
	 * `StreamChunkBatch` frames that way -- 976 bytes, four 244-byte
	 * notifications, 3 of 598 records damaged -- and the capture was
	 * reported `truncated: false`.
	 *
	 * Core reading on its own thread (embarch-core's `dev_bench_link`)
	 * removes the stall this firmware was paying for; CTS is what makes the
	 * loss structurally impossible rather than merely unlikely, since
	 * `uart_poll_out` then waits for the host instead of writing into a full
	 * buffer.
	 *
	 * The DK already assigns the pins: `uart20_default` carries UART_RTS on
	 * P1.06 and UART_CTS on P1.07 alongside TX P1.04 / RX P1.05, routed
	 * through the onboard J-Link OB to VCOM0. Nothing here rewires
	 * anything -- it only asks the UARTE to honour lines the board file
	 * already declared. */
	hw-flow-control;
};
